# Typed annotated page-set API independent review

Initial fixed snapshot: base d67e67546a0c928591e193289edb8ed702c1637e plus six recorded files. These match implementation checkpoint 7fe94c5 and merge head 197234b28095aebbe8306dd969cc9c1b420d8ba2; final-source-match.json binds each snapshot file to the merge head. The later pages.rs delta adds only two explanatory rustdoc lines about input identity; comment-delta retains exact before/after hashes and patch. No production or manuscript was edited by this reviewer. No blocking production finding was detected in this typed API scope.

## Code and contract review

The new entry takes an actual PageSet and one alias list per Doc page. It polls the caller's budget before argument validation, reexecutes domain::resolve and never accepts a caller's link-plan proof. The resolver's CheckedPages privately borrows the exact set. Its generated indices originate from the actual vectors, so the subsequent usize casts cannot introduce the external u64 truncation problem. Non-external bindings are private to the annotated implementation and are assembled separately for each source page. A same-number node from another page cannot supply its binding.

Each document still passes prepare::inspect in render_resolved. Required links must have a binding; External links still use the existing URI validator; asset/foreign and unsupported requirements do not disappear. The single-Article entry passes an empty binding table and retains NeedsResolution for Relative/Page. Source admission uses the provided codec across the whole set, including the original source/revision consistency checks. The current implementation deliberately repeats some preparation; this review does not claim a new validation-skipping optimization.

relative receives already checked ASCII artifact routes. It compares directory components, emits the necessary parent components and the unmatched target suffix, then encodes semantic fragments as n- followed by lowercase hex of exact UTF-8 bytes. It does not apply filesystem normalization or decode percent traversal. Work for input scanning and output production is charged before loops, checked size arithmetic includes parent prefixes and fragment expansion, Allocation is charged before String capacity, and the isize bound is explicit. The fragment-only helper use has a fixed root source and empty target, so its zero common prefix cannot produce an out-of-range slice. Final URI text passes the existing Markdown punctuation escape path inside angle-enclosed destinations.

All pages render before any PagesArtifact can be returned. A later invalid/unsupported page, stop, alias error or absent emitted destination discards the private outputs. Each semantic fragment must appear in the actual target's emitted-anchor inventory, in addition to the domain label check. This does not make inline Anchor or Parallel representable; those still reject in this profile. Passive PageFile bytes are not parsed and preserve Relative-no-fragment-only authority. Page target remains Doc-only. No fake target document or automatic Markdown slug is introduced.

The returned identity is explicitly the existing input PageSet identity. Aliases can change output with that identity unchanged; both the spec and the added rustdoc correctly distinguish it from a distribution artifact digest. There is no new portable PagesArtifact codec, CLI, metadata or canonical registry integration in this slice. Those future boundaries must bind aliases and renderer separately. HTML generation of passive Markdown remains unproved.

The source captured authored21 for fixed-dependency reproducibility only; this record is not an independent prose/reading review of that manuscript.

## Independent execution

The fixed isolated workspace contains 670 Git-blob dependencies plus the recorded overlay. Managed annotated_pages tests: native 5 passed. The independent public-API probe uses real named-source parser/lower for its Doc inputs and separately supplies passive Markdown bytes. Its 19 result records passed on both Windows native and wasm32-wasip2 under Wasmtime; complete result JSON is identical across targets. Probe source, exact commands, raw stdout/stderr, tool versions and binary hashes are retained.

The independent cases include seven manually expected route relationships (root, sibling, nested, reverse nested, distinct directories and deceptively similar prefixes); Japanese semantic Section ID; Ruby plus a code label containing pipe and backslash; passive file links; and mutual cross-page resolution. Destinations are checked by a separate pulldown-cmark parse, not raw escaped Markdown spelling. The target actually contains the expected n-e7af80 anchor. Aliases change output but preserve the specified set identity.

A full PageSet is encoded to canonical NDF/CBOR and decoded through a fresh FoundationCodec with an empty SourceStore and separate admission ledger. Rendering that received set yields the same set identity and exact Markdown outputs. A supplied plan with node u64::MAX fails the existing recomputing portable plan boundary. No serialized plan is ever supplied as native proof to the new renderer.

With cold admission on each operation, Work/Allocation/Output/Nodes are tested at measured successful usage minus one, preserving caller Work19; SourceBytes0 and Depth0 are separately tested. Each produces the precise expected Stopped reason, and retry keeps both reason and Usage unchanged. Cancel and caller depth7 composition pass, with depth restored afterward. Diagnostics/Events have no generated quantities in these fixtures; this is not a claim that eight resources were forced to stop.

Separately parsed documents reusing one source identity with different bytes are rejected when admitted together. A real semantic inline Anchor in a target document is rejected as Unsupported rather than generating a fictitious successful anchor destination. Non-external single-Article rendering still returns NeedsResolution. All original PageSet values remain Eq after the operations. The whole-result type and the managed later-page Parallel case preserve rejection of partial success.

Finally, real chapter13 source and alias bytes were passed to independently built before/after production executables with the same explicit input filename. The entire generated single-Article Markdown is byte-identical, including metadata. single/result.json binds both executables and all input/output hashes. This supplements the static observation that the private refactor leaves the old empty-binding path's behavior unchanged.

## Corrections and limitations

An early root test expected case-insensitive core route collision for a.md/A.md; actual PageSet contract is case-sensitive, while portable filesystem case folding belongs to the host writer. The test was corrected to identical a.md and this reviewer fixed the corrected version. No new core case-folding rule was implemented. The root's actual-01 test also initially expected a word absent from its source; the final assertion checks decoded text that actually exists. Both root failed-five logs remain in root-setup. Their failures were test expectation errors, not successful runs.

The independent probe's first build called Budget::with_depth with the wrong signature. The compiler rejected it before execution; the original source/log is retained in initial-probe. The corrected probe uses with_depth_at_least(7), the actual public API. The root's separate quoted Wasmtime runner path failure is retained as never executed; this review invokes wasmtime directly on its independently built artifact and does not count that root failure as a test pass.

No full-workspace, CLI/canonical context implementation, publication, unmigrated target HTML, human assistive-technology, or general source-less/proprietary provider acceptance is inferred. The independent probe records are concrete successful inputs and failures, not exhaustive coverage of all PageSet values. The code/spec append provides an appropriate bounded typed entry without reducing existing single-Article/HTML contracts.

Workspace, target and build caches are excluded from the evidence manifest. The snapshot, exact dependencies, private-review probe, old failed compile, commands, result logs and checksums remain. Reconstruct the fixed dependency tree before running the scripts; freeze.py captures a live tree and should not be rerun to overwrite the original evidence.
